    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d761">

                <pb facs="0905" n="761" xml:id="pg761"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">811.91293/151</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in China</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">MacMurray</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic"
                        >to the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener><seg rendition="#center">[Extracts]</seg></opener>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Peking</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1929-05-09">May 9, 1929</date>.<lb/>[Received June 11.]</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 2083</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: In reference to my telegrams No. 331, of
                    April 30, 6 p.m., and No. 362, of May 8, 5 p.m.,<anchor n="65" xml:id="d761-f1"
                    /> concerning measures taken by the Nationalist Government to bring about the
                    removal from China of certain American correspondents whose reporting has
                    apparently proved distasteful to the Government, I have, the honor to enclose a
                    copy of a confidential memorandum supplied to me on May 2nd by Mr. Hallett
                    Abend, Peking correspondent of the <hi rendition="#italic">New York
                        Times</hi>,<anchor n="66" xml:id="d761-f2"/> in reference to the Nationalist
                    Government’s alleged efforts to control the foreign press.</p>
                <p rendition="#center">. . . . . . . . . . . . . .</p>
                <p>There is also enclosed copy of a note addressed to me on May 1st by Mr. D. C.
                        Bess,<anchor n="66" xml:id="d761-f3"/> United Press representative in
                    Peking, …</p>
                <p rendition="#center">. . . . . . . . . . . . . .</p>
                <p>… From my dealings with the two correspondents named, I believe them to be
                    altogether trustworthy men, and consider that their confidential reports on the
                    foreign press situation in Shanghai deserve serious consideration.</p>
                <p>I have [etc.]</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">J. V. A.
                    MacMurray</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
